    {
      "id": "ES-C345-mismo",
      "sequence": 6170,
      "title": "mismo — the very same, from a word built by piling on emphasis",
      "headword": "mismo",
      "romanization": "mismo",
      "gloss": "same, very, self — Vulgar Latin metipsimus, a piled-up superlative of ipse self; English ipso facto and solipsism keep the ipse",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:9ef550797bd97777",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say the dozen, then double. (La docena; doble.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "otro — the word this answers."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "todo — another of the pointing words."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "MIS-mo, two beats with the weight on the first. The s stays clean and never buzzes into a z. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "mismo — \"same,\" \"very,\" \"self.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin had ipse, self, the very one. Spoken Latin decided that was not emphatic enough, and started stacking."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "First met, an emphasising particle, was stuck on the front: egomet, I myself. Then the superlative ending -issimus was piled on the back. The result was something like metipsimus — roughly the very most self-est one, a word made almost entirely of insistence."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That collapsed into Spanish mismo, French même and Italian medesimo. All three languages built their ordinary word for same out of somebody thumping a fist on the bar."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English kept ipse undisguised in a few borrowed phrases. Ipso facto is by that very fact. Solipsism is self-alone-ism, the belief that only oneself exists. And ipsissima verba are the very words themselves."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish uses mismo both ways: el mismo libro is the same book, and yo mismo is I myself."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The first word for pinning down which one."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "mismo",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *mismo*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el mismo libro",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el mismo libro*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "yo mismo",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *yo mismo*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which Latin word is under mismo? (Ipse, self.) And what does ipso facto mean? (By that very fact.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C345-cada",
      "sequence": 6180,
      "title": "cada — each, one by one, and a Greek word in plain clothes",
      "headword": "cada",
      "romanization": "cada",
      "gloss": "each, every — Greek kata, down or by, through Latin cata; English catalogue, catastrophe and cathedral all begin with the same kata",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:1e431e8ed51702b0",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say double, then same, self. (Doble; mismo.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "todo — the whole this divides."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "mismo — the other pinning word."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "KA-da, two beats with the weight on the first. The d between vowels is soft, close to the th in this. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "cada — \"each,\" \"every.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A very small, very common word, and it is Greek."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Greek katá meant down, along, by. It entered late spoken Latin in the phrase cata ūnum — one by one — which is exactly the sense cada still carries: not the whole heap at once, but taken one at a time."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That is the difference between cada and todo, which you have. Todo is the whole thing together; cada walks through it item by item."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Greek katá is at the front of a row of English words. A catalogue lists things down the page. A catastrophe is a down-turning, the moment a play overturns. A cathedral is where a bishop sits down. Catalyst, category and catapult all start the same way."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So the everyday cada día, each day, opens with the same syllable as catastrophe."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Wholes can now be walked through one at a time."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "cada",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *cada*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "cada día",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *cada día*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "cada libro",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *cada libro*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which language does cada come from? (Greek — katá.) And what is a catastrophe, word for word? (A down-turning.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C345-parte",
      "sequence": 6190,
      "title": "la parte — the part, and why a party is only a portion of the people",
      "headword": "la parte",
      "romanization": "la parte",
      "gloss": "the part, the share — Latin partem from pars; English part, party, partial, particle, partner and portion all come off it",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:cd0e1ae6d370fa67",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say same, self, then each, every. (Mismo; cada.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la mitad — one particular part."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "todo — what parts are taken from."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "PAR-te, two beats with the weight on the first. The r is a single tap, never a full trill here. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la parte — \"the part,\" \"the share.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin pars, accusative partem, meant a part, a portion, a share."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English took it and then took it again, and again, until it had a whole shelf. A part is a piece. Something partial covers only some of it. A particle is a very small piece. To participate is to take a part. A partner is one who shares a part with you. A portion is a serving. And a party is a part of the people, taken apart from the rest — which is as true of a political party as of a dinner one."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish uses it where English often reaches for a different word entirely. ¿De parte de quién? is what somebody says when answering a telephone — on whose part?, meaning who is calling? And en parte means partly."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Beside la mitad, which is a half exactly, la parte is any share at all."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Shares of a thing, not merely halves of it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la parte",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la parte*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "una parte",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *una parte*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "en parte",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *en parte*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What did partem mean? (A part, a share.) And what is a party, word for word? (A part of the people, taken apart.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C345-persona",
      "sequence": 6200,
      "title": "la persona — the person, who began as a mask",
      "headword": "la persona",
      "romanization": "la persona",
      "gloss": "the person — Latin persona, an actor's mask; English person, personality, persona and impersonate all still wear it",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:9f9173ae94e2537a",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say each, every, then the part. (Cada; la parte.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la gente — people in the mass, not one by one."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "cada — how you take them one at a time."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "per-SO-na, three beats with the weight on the middle. The r is a single tap, and the s stays clean. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la persona — \"the person.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "In Latin, a persōna was not a human being. It was a mask — the carved face a Roman actor held up or wore on stage. Probably from Etruscan, and often explained as per-sonāre, to sound through, because the mask's mouth carried the voice."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "From the mask came the part the actor played, and from the part came the human being playing it. So the ordinary word for a human being started life as the thing worn over a human face."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The mask is still visible in English. Your personality is the face you show. A persona is a mask you adopt on purpose. To impersonate is to put on somebody else's. Something personal belongs behind the mask."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Even grammar keeps it: the first, second and third person are the three masks a speaker can wear — I, you, and the one being talked about."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Beside la gente, which is people in the mass, la persona is one at a time."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "People one at a time, with a mask underneath the word."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la persona",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la persona*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "una persona",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *una persona*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "cada persona",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *cada persona*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What was a persōna in Latin? (An actor's mask.) And what are the three grammatical persons? (The three masks a speaker can wear.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C345-objeto",
      "sequence": 6210,
      "title": "el objeto — the object, thrown down in front of you",
      "headword": "el objeto",
      "romanization": "el objeto",
      "gloss": "the object, the thing — Latin objectum, thrown before, from ob and iacere to throw; English object, project, inject, eject and subject are all throws",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:5782c8697007c541",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say the part, then the person. (La parte; la persona.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la cosa — the everyday word beside this one."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la parte — another of the pinning words."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ob-HE-to, three beats with the weight on the middle. The j is the throaty jota, and the b is soft. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el objeto — \"the object,\" \"the thing.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin iacere meant to throw. Add ob-, before, against, and you get objectum — the thing thrown down in front of you. An object is whatever has been put in your way to be considered."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Iacere is one of the great English factories, and every one of its children is a throw. To project is to throw forward. To inject is to throw in. To eject is to throw out. To reject is to throw back. To subject somebody is to throw them under. To interject is to throw a remark between. A trajectory is the path of a throw. And to object to something is to throw it in the other person's path."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish has both la cosa, which is the everyday word for a thing, and el objeto, which is the deliberate one — the thing considered, the thing put in front of you on purpose."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That closes the set: mismo, cada, la parte, la persona, el objeto."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five words for pinning down which one, and the tranche is closed: thirty words across six chapters."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
